Falsifies `system_tests::user::non_volatile_memory_express_tests::a_confined_el0_process_serves_the_block_interface_end_to_end`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the block-to-LBA conversion in `Handoff::transfer_command` dropped: the server sends
a filesystem block number to the controller as if it were a logical block address, so block 37
starts at LBA 37 rather than LBA `37 * blocks_per`. This is the classic factor-of-8 confusion
between a 4096-byte filesystem block and a 512-byte LBA, and it is the exact failure the test's own
comment on block 37 says it is placed to catch. The size, the range check, the PRP pair and the
completion path all still work, so the server comes up, reports the right geometry and answers
every verb; it just writes where it was not told to.

The test needs an NVMe controller attached, which `cargo xtask test` does on every test leg
(`NIFE_NVME`), so this replays with no extra setup. Without one the test SKIPS and the sweep
reports an error rather than a result.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(*b, pattern(i), "byte {i} of block {block} came back wrong")`.
Block 37 lands on LBAs 37 to 44 and block 38 on LBAs 38 to 45, so the second write overwrites
seven eighths of the first and the first read-back of block 37 comes back wrong at byte 512.

This replaces the two hand falsifications of milestone 318 (the NVMe boot test on real geometry) as the replayable record. Those were edits
to the test; this is a defect in the code the test exists to check.
